Subject: [PATCH 15/71] docs: Record the MXM1120 protocol and the elevator
 hardware
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

s6patch霍尔/README.md now documents what the second half of the reverse
engineering recovered:

 - the MXM1120 i2c protocol, its register map, the verbatim probe sequence
   (including the device id check against 0x9c) and the measurement decoding for
   both the 10-bit and the 8-bit mode, together with the fact that the factory
   input devices are named m1120_up and m1120_down with ABS_X -32768..32767 and
   BUS_I2C, and the four device tree properties the vendor driver parsed;
 - the elevator motor hardware from the factory device tree: the five gpios
   (boost, enable, sleep, dir and id), the fact that there is no pwm controller
   and that the drive waveform is the generic purpose clock gcc_gp2_clk routed
   out on gpio21 by the "vib_pwm_active" pinctrl state, that GCC_GP2_CLK is
   clock id 36 and that its frequency table only offers 19.2, 25, 50, 100 and
   200 MHz, and that "vib_pwm_idconfig" pulls gpio25 up to read the id strap;
 - the strings recovered from bbk_hall_core, which name the four calibration
   ioctls (SET_CALI, SET_MHALL_CALI, SET_MHALL_CALI_DAEMON and TRANS_CALI) and
   the persist files cali_hall and cali_mhall_final;
 - two more reverse engineering recipes that made this possible: resolving a
   string constant from its adrp/add pair and dumping it with objdump -s, and
   decoding the struct device_attribute and struct file_operations objects in
   .data to get straight to the handlers.

